{
  "image_name": "41567_2025_3001_Fig3_HTML.webp",
  "prompt_text": "The figure organizes different kinds of chiral and axial phonons by whether atomic motion breaks symmetry and whether the vibration carries angular momentum. At the top, it separates “chiral phonons,” which break improper rotational symmetry, from “axial phonons,” which carry angular momentum \\(J\\). These ideas overlap but are not identical.\n\nThe left side shows geometric chiral phonons: handed lattice distortions that are mirror-related left and right forms. These can be handed even when the phonon angular momentum is zero, emphasizing that chirality can come from the displacement pattern itself rather than from circular motion. A contrasting nonhanded example also has \\(J=0\\).\n\nThe center and right emphasize circular atomic motion. Helical phonons are labeled as axial chiral: the circular motion and propagation direction \\(k\\) combine so that \\(k \\cdot J \\neq 0\\), giving a handed, symmetry-breaking mode. In contrast, cycloidal phonons and circular \\(\\Gamma\\)-point phonons are axial but achiral: they carry angular momentum, yet the geometry retains symmetry, with \\(k \\cdot J = 0\\) or \\(k=0\\).\n\nOverall, the diagram clarifies that phonon chirality, handedness, and angular momentum are related but distinct concepts.",
